riepilogo:
Chapter 14 «Atlante dei linguaggi» is written, the first-round lab «L'albero dei linguaggi» has been checked and fixed, and its tests are new. All 14 tests in atlante.test.js pass, and chapter 14 passes dati.test.js.

1) Chapter 14 (js/capitoli/14-atlante.js), replacing the stub. I kept the fields from the spec's «Keep» line (id, numero, anni, anno, speciale, etichetta, titolo, sottotitolo), with no epoca and no ciao. The text is 694 words, counting the intro, the table and «Lo sapevi?». Blocks:
- testo «Come si legge la mappa»: how to read the map, why languages borrow from each other, what a paradigm is, and the link to hardware (von Neumann architecture, machine code, logic gates).
- lab atlante with largo: true, plus a title and an intro.
- tabella of 7 paradigms (paradigma, idea in una frase, linguaggi d'esempio), with glossary links in the first column.
- testo «Nessun linguaggio va bene per tutto»: typing, Backus's 1977 Turing lecture against «linguaggi di von Neumann» and the «collo di bottiglia», and why no language is best for everything. It ends with Perlis's epigram 19.
- sapevi: languages named after people, and Lua / SOL.

Glossary terms defined here: paradigma di programmazione, programmazione imperativa, programmazione funzionale, tipizzazione. It also uses terms owned by other chapters (compilatore, interprete, codice macchina, memoria, architettura di von Neumann, garbage collection, variabile, programmazione strutturata, programmazione a oggetti, programmazione logica, programmazione dichiarativa, linguaggio di scripting, intelligenza artificiale), and all of them are now defined. Internal links go to #logica, #cavi, #assembly, #ia and #telai; links to other sections go to ../porte-logiche/ and ../chip-programmabili/. There are 8 sources.

2) New test file (test/atlante.test.js), 14 tests:
- at least 50 languages, including the 50 the spec names;
- unique ids and names;
- every influence exists, is not repeated, does not point to itself, and there are at most 4 per language;
- no influencer is younger than the language it influenced;
- no cycles, plus a check that the cycle detector finds a cycle in made-up data;
- every chapter id is valid, and the lab's fallback chapter titles match the real chapter files (loaded with node:vm);
- every language has a hello world, or a written reason why it has none (only for languages before 1965);
- valid fields and paradigms, no HTML or straight double quotes in the texts, descriptions of 1–3 sentences;
- ancestors and descendants, including their distance in generations and that the two directions agree;
- search, filter and sorting;
- the map layout: labels in the same row never overlap, every label stays inside its lane, one curve per influence;
- the curves and the time slider.

3) Lab fixes (js/lab/atlante.js). The tests found no data errors. The browser showed one real bug: css/style.css gives every table `min-width: 1050px`, so the list view overflowed inside its box (by 660 px at 390 wide, 90 px at 1440) and the Anno column was out of sight on phones. I fixed it inside the lab with `.lab-atlante-tab { min-width: 0 }`. Other changes:
- The hidden table caption can no longer widen the table.
- Below 560 px the list has no inner vertical scroll, and the list no longer traps the page scroll.
- The Plankalkül footnote only shows when Plankalkül is in the filtered list.
- Clearer legend: «discendenti: quelli che ha ispirato».
- BASIC now links to chapter «personal» (which has the BASIC lab and the most coverage), and AWK links to «unix».
- Small wording fixes in the Self and PHP descriptions.

limiti:
- **No online fact-checking this round.** The web search allowance shared by all agents had run out, and the proxy refused Wikipedia, wikiquote, lua.org and cs.yale.edu (connect_rejected), so no fetch worked either. Every fact in the new chapter and in the lab data was checked only against my own knowledge.
- **Items the reviewer should verify online first:**
  - the exact wording of Perlis's epigram 19 and my Italian translation;
  - Lua / SOL;
  - whether `outstring` in the ALGOL 60 hello world comes from the 1976 Modified Report;
  - the B tutorial date (1972 or 1973);
  - Self, designed in 1986 and presented in 1987;
  - the URLs in fonti (written from memory, not opened).
- **Not changed:** I did not review the rest of the lab's 58 descriptions and hello worlds line by line against sources. They looked right on a careful read, and the reviewer will check them.
- **Entrance animation:** the opening time sweep lasts about 6.5 s. Languages appear only when the cursor reaches their year, so in automated tests a click right after the page loads can time out. A real user who touches the map stops the sweep.
